import "../../../../sass/accessibility.scss";
import { urls } from "../../../../data";

<div class="a11y-pattern-content">
  <h1>Calendar Accessibility</h1>
  <h2>Best Practices</h2>
  <p>
    The calendar <strong>must</strong> be structured as a data table. Use
    <code>&lt;caption&gt;</code> for the month and year, and column headers (
    <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> ) for the days of the week. This gives assistive
    technology the same table navigation commands available on any other data
    table.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only use the <em>interactive</em> calendar (date buttons) when the user is
    actually selecting a date. A read-only calendar — one used purely to display
    a date or range — <strong>should not</strong> expose its dates as buttons;
    use plain text, or a link if the date itself is actionable (e.g. "view
    orders placed on this date").
  </p>
  <p>
    Date state (today, selected, start of range, in range, end of range,
    disabled) <strong>must not</strong> be conveyed by color or border alone.
    Pair every visual state with a programmatic equivalent: the interactive
    calendar uses <code>aria-current="date"</code> for today,
    <code>aria-pressed="true"</code> for a selected date, the native
    <code>disabled</code> attribute for unavailable dates, and composes only the
    range text (start, in, end of range) into each date button's
    <code>aria-label</code>. The read-only calendar appends visually-hidden text alongside
    the visible day number.
  </p>
  <p>
    When focused, the previous/next month buttons <strong>must</strong> have an
    accessible label and <strong>should</strong> name the direction and the
    month being navigated to, such as "Previous month, August 2026" and "Next
    month, September 2026".
  </p>
  <h2>Interaction Design</h2>
  <p>
    This section provides detailed instructions for how different input types
    should navigate and operate the pattern.
  </p>
  <h3>Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    For the <em>interactive</em> calendar, date buttons use a
    <a href=`${urls.accessibility}/techniques/roving-tabindex`>
      roving tabindex
    </a>
    . Only one date (the current or most recently focused date) is in the page's
    <strong>TAB</strong> sequence at a time; every other date button has
    <code>tabindex="-1"</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    With focus on a date, <strong>RIGHT-ARROW</strong> and
    <strong>LEFT-ARROW</strong> keys <strong>must</strong> move focus one day
    forward/backward. <strong>DOWN-ARROW</strong> and <strong>UP-ARROW</strong>
    keys <strong>must</strong> move focus one week forward/backward. Disabled
    dates <strong>must</strong> be skipped over, never landed on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>HOME</strong> and <strong>END</strong> keys move focus to the first
    and last enabled date currently visible. <strong>PAGE-UP</strong> and
    <strong>PAGE-DOWN</strong> keys move focus to the same weekday in the
    previous/next month; for a <em>navigable</em> calendar this also changes the
    visible month.
  </p>
  <p>
    If arrow, Home, End, Page Up, or Page Down navigation would move focus
    outside the currently visible month(s) of a <em>navigable</em> calendar, the
    calendar <strong>must</strong> advance to the adjacent month and move focus
    to the corresponding date there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>SPACEBAR</strong> or <strong>ENTER</strong> key on a focused date
    <strong>must</strong> select that date. When the calendar supports a range,
    selecting a second date after a first <strong>must</strong> create a range
    between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The previous/next month buttons, when present, sit in the normal document
    tab order — <em>outside</em> of the date grid's roving tabindex — and behave
    like any other <a href=`${urls.components}/icon-button`>icon button</a> .
  </p>
  <h3>Screen Reader</h3>
  <p>
    The month and year <strong>must</strong> be announced via the table's
    <code>&lt;caption&gt;</code> when the calendar is discovered, or when the
    virtual cursor enters the table.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each day of the week <strong>must</strong> be announced as a column header
    when a screen reader moves between date cells, per standard data table
    semantics.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the interactive calendar, a focused date button announces its day
    number, its selected state (via <code>aria-pressed</code>) and whether it is today
    (via <code>aria-current</code>), plus any range text ("start of range", "in
    range", "end of range") composed into the button's <code>aria-label</code> .
  </p>
  <p>
    For the read-only calendar, the same state text is appended as
    visually-hidden text alongside the visible day number, rather than via
    <code>aria-label</code> , since these dates may also be links.
  </p>
  <h3>Pointer</h3>
  <p>
    Clicking or tapping a date button <strong>must</strong> select that date.
    Clicking or tapping a disabled date <strong>must</strong> have no effect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clicking or tapping a previous/next month button <strong>must</strong>
    navigate the calendar by one month.
  </p>
  <h2>ARIA Reference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Attribute</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>aria-label</strong></td>
        <td>
          Applied to the previous/next month buttons to announce which month
          they navigate to. Applied to an interactive date button, in place of
          its visible day number, when that date is part of a range, to append
          the range state text (start, in, end of range).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>aria-current="date"</strong></td>
        <td>Applied to today's date button, when interactive.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>aria-pressed</strong></td>
        <td>
          Applied to an interactive date button to convey its selected state.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>tabindex</strong></td>
        <td>
          Set to <code>0</code> on exactly one interactive date button (the
          roving tabindex) and <code>-1</code> on all others.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>Further Reading</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href=`${urls.accessibility}/techniques/roving-tabindex`>
        Roving Tabindex
      </a>
    </li>
    <li><a href=`${urls.components}/date-textbox`>Date Textbox</a></li>
  </ul>
</div>
